8a898d3e66735dcec"},"seconds":13.035887956619263,"seed":16078379984643139,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the value of the constant \\(a\\) for which the given system of equations has infinitely many solutions, we need to analyze the conditions under which the two equations represent the same line. The system of equations is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n2x + 5y &= -8, \\tag{1} \\\\\n6x &= 16 + a - 15y. \\tag{2}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rewrite equation (2) in the standard form \\(Ax + By = C\\). Starting with equation (2):\n\n\\[\n6x = 16 + a - 15y,\n\\]\n\nwe rearrange the terms to isolate the variables on one side:\n\n\\[\n6x + 15y = 16 + a. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we have the system:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n2x + 5y &= -8, \\tag{1} \\\\\n6x + 15y &= 16 + a. \\tag{3}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFor the system to have infinitely many solutions, the two equations must be proportional. This means that the coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in both equations must be proportional, and the constants on the right-hand side must also be proportional. Specifically, the ratios of the coefficients of \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and the constants must be equal. That is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{5}{15} = \\frac{-8}{16 + a}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the first two ratios:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{5}{15} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSo, we need:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{-8}{16 + a}.\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to solve for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\cdot (16 + a) = 3 \\cdot (-8),\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n16 + a = -24.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\na = -24 - 16 = -40.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the system of equations have infinitely many solutions is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-40}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-40}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:585"}
